@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# Slow pantry search
+
+For the volunteer investigating slow item searches, including Saturday morning load.
+The repository has no production query plans or schema history, so a missing index is a hypothesis,
+not a confirmed cause. The query in `larder/catalog.py` already uses Postgres full-text search.
+
+## Inspect before changing
+
+Connect to the app's Postgres database with `psql`. Inspect the existing indexes:
+
+```sql
+SELECT indexrelid::regclass AS index_name, indisvalid, pg_get_indexdef(indexrelid)
+FROM pg_index
+WHERE indrelid = 'pantry_items'::regclass;
+```
+
+Record the following plan and execution time with representative member searches and during representative
+load. Replace `'rice'` with those terms. `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` executes the read query.
+
+```sql
+EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
+SELECT id, name, stock FROM pantry_items
+WHERE to_tsvector('simple', name) @@ plainto_tsquery('simple', 'rice')
+ORDER BY name;
+```
+
+## Apply the index if missing
+
+If a valid GIN index on `to_tsvector('simple', name)` already exists, skip the migration and investigate
+the plan, database load and lock waits. Do not create another equivalent index with a different name.
+Otherwise, from the repository root, using the normal database connection environment:
+
+```sh
+psql -X -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f migrations/001_pantry_items_name_search.sql
+```
+
+Run outside a transaction; do not use `psql --single-transaction`. Schedule the build outside pantry
+opening hours because it adds database work. The concurrent build allows item writes to continue.
+There is no migration runner in this repository; this script is applied manually once.
+
+The expression matches the existing query exactly, as required by
+[Postgres expression-index matching](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/textsearch-tables.html).
+Search results, name ordering and reads of current stock keep their existing behavior.
+
+## Verify and recover
+
+Repeat the index inspection and the same query plans after the build. Expect a valid index and compare
+execution time and buffers against the baseline. Postgres may still choose a sequential scan for a small
+table or a common term. Do not force index use or claim a speedup without representative measurements.
+If the page remains slow, use those measurements to investigate further before adding a service.
+
+A failed concurrent build can leave an invalid index. Check `indisvalid` before retrying; a name
+collision must not silently skip a failed build. To remove this migration's index for recovery or rollback:
+
+```sql
+DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY pantry_items_name_search_idx;
+```
+
+Run that outside a transaction, then rerun the migration if appropriate. See
+[Postgres concurrent index builds](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createindex.html#SQL-CREATEINDEX-CONCURRENTLY)
+for build limitations and failure recovery.
+
+## Why this change uses Postgres
+
+The [service rule](../architecture.md#before-adding-a-service) requires both a capability Postgres cannot
+provide and two volunteers able to restore a new service. The Redis request names only Tom and supplies
+no evidence that Postgres cannot meet this need. Previous pantry search work in the architecture page
+also succeeded with Postgres. This change prepares an index for the existing query without introducing
+a cache of stock values or another service to operate. Redis would need a proposal satisfying both
+conditions before adoption.
